<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<!--
  Fast-DDS participant profile for large sensor_msgs/Image streams (6.2 MB raw 1080p frames).

  Loaded through FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE (the distro's rmw_fastrtps_cpp creates every
  participant of a process from the default profile):
    * scripts\publish_video_file.py sets it in-process before rclpy.init (the SENDING side, where it
      matters; unless the variable is already set in the environment),
    * scripts\run_live_rviz_stream_real.ps1 exports it for rviz2 (RViz's own Image display and the
      plugin's AR video reader are two participants of that process) so both ends agree.
  To check that it is active, while a sending process runs (a directory listing shows 0 for open
  files, so open them):
    Get-ChildItem C:\ProgramData\eprosima\fastrtps_interprocess | ? { $_.Name -notmatch '_el$|_mutex$|port' } |
      % { $f = [IO.File]::Open($_.FullName,'Open','Read','ReadWrite,Delete'); '{0} {1:N0}' -f $_.Name,$f.Length; $f.Dispose() }
  -> 67,133,984 bytes (64 MB) for a participant that uses it, 549,408 (512 KB) for one that does not.

  What it changes, and why (the measurements are in publish_video_file.py's docstring):
    * The shared-memory (SHM) transport sends through ONE segment per sending participant, 512 KB
      by default in Fast-DDS 2.6, while a 6.2 MB frame is ~96 fragments of 64 KB. With the default
      segment 5-8 % of the frames lose fragments (a best-effort subscriber simply misses them), and
      the reliable protocol brings them back 10-25 ms late - the SAME frames at EVERY subscriber,
      which is why RViz's Image display and the AR panel stutter together once both are
      subscribed. A 64 MB segment (~10 whole frames) loses nothing.
    * The segment is a file of that size per participant, created while the process runs and
      removed when it exits cleanly; a process that dies hard leaves it behind until
      `fastdds shm clean` (the launcher runs that at every start) removes it.
    * UDPv4 stays enabled at its defaults, so discovery, remote peers and readers that cannot use
      shared memory behave as with the stock profile. (A UDP-only profile is also smooth and
      lossless, but publish() takes ~10 ms instead of ~6 ms. Bigger UDP socket buffers make no
      measurable difference locally; a remote peer on a lossy link may want
      sendBufferSize/receiveBufferSize.)
-->
<profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
  <transport_descriptors>
    <transport_descriptor>
      <transport_id>qar_shm_large</transport_id>
      <type>SHM</type>
      <segment_size>67108864</segment_size>
    </transport_descriptor>
    <transport_descriptor>
      <transport_id>qar_udp</transport_id>
      <type>UDPv4</type>
    </transport_descriptor>
  </transport_descriptors>

  <participant profile_name="qar_large_data" is_default_profile="true">
    <rtps>
      <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
      <userTransports>
        <transport_id>qar_shm_large</transport_id>
        <transport_id>qar_udp</transport_id>
      </userTransports>
    </rtps>
  </participant>
</profiles>
